    body: `<p>The word arrived with the force of a confession. Exvangelical. It surfaced on social media around 2016, coined by Blake Chastain as a podcast title, but the reality it named had been gathering for at least a decade before that. People were leaving evangelical churches in numbers that could no longer be dismissed as backsliding or cultural drift. They were leaving deliberately, articulately, and often in tremendous pain. They were not becoming atheists, most of them. They were becoming something the evangelical framework had no category for: Christians who could no longer remain in the tradition that had formed them.</p>

<p>The distinction is essential and almost always collapsed. To leave evangelicalism is not to leave Christianity. It is to leave a particular institutional and theological expression of Christianity — one that, for many who have departed, had become so identified with political power, cultural rigidity, and institutional self-protection that it no longer resembled the faith it claimed to represent. The conflation of leaving a tradition with leaving the faith is itself one of evangelicalism's most damaging habits of thought. It assumes that evangelicalism is Christianity's final and most faithful form, and that departure from it is departure from God. That assumption is historically illiterate and theologically unsustainable, but it remains the operating framework for millions of American Christians.</p>

<p>To understand the exvangelical movement, you must first understand what they are leaving and why the leaving carries such weight.</p>

<h2>What Evangelicalism Became</h2>

<p>American evangelicalism, as a coherent movement, is younger than most of its adherents realize. The National Association of Evangelicals was founded in 1942. The movement coalesced in the postwar period as a deliberate alternative to both mainline Protestantism, which was seen as theologically compromised, and fundamentalism, which was seen as culturally disengaged. Figures like Billy Graham, Carl F.H. Henry, and Harold Ockenga sought a third way: theologically conservative but intellectually engaged, culturally present but not combative.</p>

<p>That project held for roughly two generations. By the late 1970s, the movement had begun its fateful merger with political conservatism. Jerry Falwell's Moral Majority, founded in 1979, was the visible expression of a deeper shift: evangelicalism was no longer merely a theological identity. It was becoming a political one. The Reagan coalition welded together evangelical piety, free-market economics, and Cold War nationalism into a package that was presented as Christian faithfulness but was, in historical terms, a contingent political alliance with no necessary theological basis.</p>

<p>By the time the children of that alliance reached adulthood, many of them could not tell where the theology ended and the politics began. That was not an accident. It was the point.</p>

<p>The exvangelical movement is composed largely of people who grew up inside this synthesis and eventually found it impossible to maintain. They are the homeschooled children of the culture wars, the youth group alumni who were told that voting Republican was a Christian obligation, the worship leaders who watched their churches become arms of a political movement and were told that objecting to that development was a sign of weak faith.</p>

<h2>The Catalysts</h2>

<p>No single event produced the exvangelical exodus, but several converging crises accelerated it beyond what the movement's institutions could absorb.</p>

<p>The first was the abuse crisis. Beginning in the early 2010s and accelerating through the rest of the decade, a cascade of revelations exposed systematic patterns of sexual abuse, institutional cover-up, and leadership failure across evangelical organizations. The Southern Baptist Convention's abuse scandal, documented in devastating detail by the Houston Chronicle's 2019 investigation and the subsequent Guidepost Solutions report, revealed that denominational leaders had maintained a list of credibly accused abusers for years while publicly insisting they had no mechanism to track such cases. Ravi Zacharias, one of evangelicalism's most celebrated apologists, was posthumously revealed to have been a serial sexual predator. Bill Hybels, founder of Willow Creek and architect of the seeker-sensitive movement, resigned amid multiple allegations of harassment and abuse. Mark Driscoll, Mars Hill Church. James MacDonald, Harvest Bible Chapel. The list grew so long that it ceased to function as a list of exceptions and began to function as an indictment of the system itself.</p>

<p>For many exvangelicals, the abuse revelations were not merely about individual failures. They were about a theology of authority that made those failures predictable. Complementarian gender theology, pastoral authoritarianism, the absence of meaningful accountability structures, the prioritization of institutional reputation over victim safety — these were not bugs in the evangelical system. They were features. And the people who named them as such were the ones who were shown the door.</p>

<p>The second catalyst was the LGBTQ+ question. As American culture moved rapidly toward acceptance of same-sex relationships, evangelical churches doubled down on exclusion with a ferocity that many younger members found incompatible with the Jesus they had been taught to follow. The issue was not merely theological disagreement about sexual ethics. It was the pastoral cruelty that accompanied the disagreement: conversion therapy, family rejection, the casual dehumanization of queer people from the pulpit, the refusal to engage with the growing body of affirming biblical scholarship, and the willingness to make opposition to LGBTQ+ rights a defining marker of Christian identity.</p>

<p>For parents of queer children, for closeted members sitting in pews, for allies who had watched their friends be wounded, the cost of staying became higher than the cost of leaving. Many report that the moment they could no longer remain was not an intellectual moment but a moral one: they could not, in conscience, participate in an institution that treated people they loved as inherently disordered.</p>

<p>The third catalyst was racial reckoning. The killings of Michael Brown, Eric Garner, Breonna Taylor, and George Floyd forced a national conversation about race that evangelical churches were catastrophically unprepared for. The movement's history on race is damning: the Southern Baptist Convention was founded in 1845 explicitly to defend the right of slaveholders to serve as missionaries. Bob Jones University maintained its ban on interracial dating until 2000. The overwhelming whiteness of evangelical leadership, the persistent segregation of Sunday morning, the movement's deep roots in the theology of the Lost Cause — all of this was suddenly, unavoidably visible.</p>

<p>Black and brown evangelicals who raised these issues were met with a response that was revealing: they were told they were being divisive. Critical race theory became the new enemy, a label applied so broadly and indiscriminately that it functioned less as a description of an academic framework and more as a mechanism for silencing any conversation about structural racism. For many Christians of color and their white allies, the message was clear: this movement values its racial comfort more than its commitment to justice. And so they left.</p>

<p>The fourth catalyst was the Trump era. The 2016 election and its aftermath did not create the contradictions within evangelicalism, but it made them impossible to ignore. When eighty-one percent of white evangelicals voted for a thrice-married casino magnate who bragged about sexual assault, many younger evangelicals experienced a dissonance so severe that it fractured their relationship with the movement permanently. It was not merely that evangelical leaders endorsed Trump. It was that they did so enthusiastically, that they provided theological justification for it, that they compared him to Cyrus and Nehemiah, that they treated criticism of him as persecution, and that they did all of this while claiming to represent the moral center of American life.</p>

<p>The hypocrisy was not new. But its scale was.</p>

<h2>Where They Go</h2>

<p>The assumption that exvangelicals become secularists is widespread and wrong. Research by the Public Religion Research Institute and other organizations consistently shows that the majority of people who leave evangelicalism do not abandon faith entirely. They migrate.</p>

<p>Some move to mainline Protestant traditions — Episcopal, ELCA Lutheran, Presbyterian (PCUSA), United Methodist — drawn by liturgical richness, intellectual freedom, and a theology that does not demand the subordination of conscience to institutional authority. They find in these traditions a Christianity that can hold ambiguity, that has a longer memory than the evangelical movement, that does not treat doubt as disloyalty.</p>

<p>Some move to Roman Catholicism or Eastern Orthodoxy, seeking rootedness in a tradition older than the Reformation, a sacramental theology that locates God's presence in material reality rather than in emotional experience alone, and a theological depth that evangelicalism's pragmatic orientation often sacrificed for accessibility.</p>

<p>Some construct what the sociologist Nancy Ammerman calls "a la carte" spirituality — drawing from contemplative traditions, Celtic Christianity, liberation theology, and other sources that evangelicalism had dismissed or never engaged. They read Richard Rohr and Howard Thurman. They practice lectio divina and the Examen. They attend Quaker meetings or sit in centering prayer groups. They are not less Christian. They are differently Christian, and the difference is one their former communities cannot process.</p>

<p>And some, it must be said, leave religion entirely. The wounds are too deep, the associations too painful, the word "God" too contaminated by the uses to which it has been put. For these, the departure is not a theological conclusion but a survival mechanism. They are not rejecting the divine. They are protecting themselves from the human institutions that claimed to represent it. Whether they return is a question only grace and time can answer.</p>

<p>What is striking across all of these trajectories is how few exvangelicals describe their departure as a loss of faith. The language they use is more precise than that: deconstruction, reconstruction, untangling, sifting. They are not throwing away the faith. They are separating what they actually believe from what they were told they had to believe — separating Jesus from the Jesus Industrial Complex, the scriptures from the hermeneutic that was imposed upon them, the genuine encounter with God they experienced in prayer or worship from the institutional machinery that surrounded and claimed ownership of that encounter. This is not apostasy. It is curation under duress, performed without a guide, often at great personal cost.</p>

<p>The data supports this reading. Ryan Burge's research on the "nones" — those who claim no religious affiliation — reveals that a significant subset of this population maintains private spiritual practices, believes in God or a higher power, and would return to organized religion if they could find a community that did not replicate the patterns they left. They are, in a meaningful sense, believers without belonging, and their existence is an indictment not of belief itself but of the institutions that made belonging unbearable.</p>

<h2>The Grief of Departure</h2>

<p>What outsiders rarely grasp is the cost. Leaving evangelicalism is not like switching political parties or changing gyms. For people raised in the tradition, evangelicalism was not one part of their identity. It was the organizing principle of their entire world. Their friendships were formed there. Their marriages were blessed there. Their children were dedicated there. Their moral framework, their understanding of history, their conception of what happens after death — all of it was sourced from a community they are now leaving.</p>

<p>The grief is compound. There is the loss of community, which for many was the only genuine community they had ever known. There is the loss of certainty, which for people raised in a tradition that prizes doctrinal confidence feels like vertigo. There is the loss of family relationships, because in many evangelical families, leaving the church is treated as a betrayal equivalent to moral failure, and the response — shunning, conditional love, relentless attempts at re-conversion — can be devastating.</p>

<p>There is also a specific grief that is harder to name: the grief of realizing that the story you were told about yourself and the world was, in significant ways, wrong. That the church you trusted protected abusers. That the theology you internalized was designed to control you. That the God you were given was too small, too angry, too American, too white, too male, too invested in your compliance and not enough in your flourishing. Reconstructing a faith after that realization is not a weekend project. It is the work of years, and it is done largely without institutional support, because the institution you left was the only one you knew.</p>

<p>And there is the grief of language itself. The words that once carried transcendence — worship, fellowship, salvation, blessing — now arrive pre-contaminated, triggering associations with manipulation, performance, and control. The exvangelical does not merely lose a community. They lose a vocabulary. They must learn to speak about God, about meaning, about hope, in a language that has not been weaponized against them. Some find that language in the contemplative tradition, in the mystics, in poets. Some find it in therapy. Some simply go silent for a season, unable to pray in the old words and not yet fluent in new ones. That silence is not the absence of faith. It is faith in transit, between the language it was given and the language it has not yet found.</p>

<p>The psychologist Marlene Winell coined the term "Religious Trauma Syndrome" to describe the cluster of symptoms common among people leaving authoritarian religious environments: anxiety, depression, difficulty making decisions, a pervasive sense of being watched or judged, the inability to trust one's own perceptions. Winell's work, and the growing body of clinical literature on religious trauma, confirms what exvangelicals have been saying for years: leaving is not merely an intellectual adjustment. It is a psychological upheaval comparable in its effects to leaving any high-control environment. The church that produced the trauma rarely acknowledges it, which means the healing must happen entirely outside the system that inflicted the wound.</p>

<h2>What the Church Must Learn</h2>

<p>The evangelical church's dominant response to the exvangelical movement has been dismissal. They were never really saved. They loved the world more than God. They wanted to sin without guilt. This response is pastorally catastrophic and historically oblivious. It is the response of an institution that cannot distinguish between loyalty to itself and faithfulness to God.</p>

<p>The prophetic question is not "why are they leaving" but "what are they telling us by leaving." The exvangelical exodus is a prophetic indictment delivered not in words but in feet — people walking out the door because the institution no longer embodies the gospel it preaches. The church that cannot hear that indictment is the church that will keep producing it.</p>

<p>What would it mean to listen? It would mean taking the abuse crisis seriously — not as a public relations problem but as a theological failure that reveals something rotten in the movement's understanding of authority and power. It would mean engaging the LGBTQ+ conversation with genuine theological humility rather than treating the traditional position as self-evident and every other position as capitulation. It would mean reckoning with the movement's racial history honestly, without the defensive posture that treats acknowledging complicity as attacking the faith. It would mean disentangling the gospel from partisan politics with enough conviction that a visitor could attend an evangelical church for a year and never know how the pastor voted.</p>

<p>It would mean, in short, repentance. Not the performative kind that issues statements. The structural kind that changes how power works.</p>

<blockquote>The church that treats its critics as enemies will eventually have no one left but sycophants. The church that treats its departures as data — painful, prophetic data about its own failures — has a chance of becoming something worth staying in.</blockquote>

<p>The exvangelicals are not the church's enemies. They are its mirror. And the reflection, for anyone willing to look, is a call not to defensiveness but to the kind of reform that the church has always needed and has always resisted — until the cost of resistance became higher than the cost of change.</p>

<p>That cost is now being counted in empty pews, in broken families, in a generation that learned the word "Christian" as a synonym for hypocrisy and is not wrong about why. The question is whether the evangelical church can hear that without hearing it as an attack. The answer to that question will determine whether what is happening is a death or a reformation.</p>

<p>The difference between the two has always depended on whether the institution loves its own survival more than it loves the truth. And the truth, as always, is not comfortable. It is not affirming. It is not what the institution wants to hear. But it is, if received, the only thing that can set it free.</p>`
